WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.240
Marvel's Avengers: Infinity War packed a lot
of characters and a lot of plot into its nearly

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:05.920
three hour runtime.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.460
With all the character names, subplots, and
relationships that fans had to keep straight,

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.459
it's no surprise that there were some Easter
eggs that flew over audiences' heads.

00:00:14.459 --> 00:00:18.920
Here are a few small details you might have
missed in Avengers: Infinity War.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.060
"Nothing goes over my head.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.940
My reflexes are too fast.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.200
I would catch it."

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.320
Nebula disassembled

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:31.260
Nebula's torture at the hands of Thanos is
both one of the more gruesome parts of the

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:36.360
movie, and a reference to the Infinity Gauntlet
comic arc that provides the inspiration for

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.690
much of the film.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:42.579
While the movie showcases Nebula strung up
like a Damien Hirst exhibit, the comic series

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:48.630
features Thanos reducing her to a brain-dead
zombie-like form, but still pretty much keeping

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.329
her on display for company.

00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:55.640
Her eventual counter-attack leads to her possessing
the Infinity Gauntlet, which she uses to restore

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.610
the lives that Thanos ended.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:02.140
Don't be surprised if she has a big role in
restoring the lives of Earth's Mightiest Heroes

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:02.680
next year.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.060
Ultimate Thor

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:10.399
When Thor returns to Earth with his new hammer,
Stormbreaker, it's a thrilling moment for

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.560
comic fans and audience members alike.

00:01:13.109 --> 00:01:17.780
What some fans might not have noticed is that
the visuals of Thor's return are straight

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.220
out of The Ultimates comic series.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:24.920
Both the glowing bulbs of his vest and his
axe-like hammer are pulled directly from the

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.200
Ultimate Universe of Marvel Comics.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.720
Here's hoping that the new weapon fares a
bit better than Thor's old hammer.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.600
Cap v Thanos: Dawn of Just Us

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.360
There's plenty of moments in the Infinity
Gauntlet comics that aren't adapted for Infinity

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.740
War, but there's one moment in the comic series
that absolutely had to be adapted: Captain

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.780
America facing down Thanos after watching
him beat down the strongest heroes in the

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.200
Marvel universe without breaking a sweat.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.400
It's a character-defining moment for Steve
Rogers as the last Avenger standing, and the

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.820
spirit of that moment makes it into Infinity
War beautifully.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.020
Colorblind Vision

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.220
In Infinity War, when Thanos finally rips
the Mind Stone out of Vision's head, his body

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:13.540
immediately loses coloring.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:19.300
Vision's newly colorless body is pulled straight
from the comics; specifically, West Coast

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:19.800
Avengers.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.640
After getting his programming wiped, Vision
returned to functionality as a fully colorless,

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.660
much less emotional hero than he had been.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.940
Infinity War's reference to Vision losing
color after getting his mind destroyed is

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.590
likely an indication that even if Vision gets
resurrected with some of his teammates, he

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.720
probably won't be the same robot that fans
know and love.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.540
"I am not JARVIS."

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.060
I am."

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.000
17A protocol

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:55.870
When Tony Stark orders the 17A protocol,
it gives Peter Parker the high-tech Iron Spider

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.560
outfit that Spidey uses to great effect.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.520
It's a crowd-pleasing moment, but the quick
reference to Spider-Man and the number "17"

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.400
might have left well-read comic fans a bit
nervous.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.530
In Spider-Man #17, Peter Parker gets killed
while trying to save a woman and her daughter

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.719
from a vat of exploding Freon.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:17.380
In the afterlife, Spidey meets Thanos and
his beloved Lady Death.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.090
After a struggle, Death takes pity on Spider-Man
and the young girl he failed to save, allowing

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:24.360
them to come back to life.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.140
Like in the comic, Spidey doesn't survive
through infinity War; but it's likely that

00:03:29.141 --> 00:03:33.120
he'll come back in the sequel, since comic
books rarely keep anyone off the pages for

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:33.640
long.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.500
That's a lot packed into a blink-and-you'll-miss-it
reference!

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.160
The Doctor is in trouble

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.140
Early in the film, Dr. Strange is captured
by Ebony Maw, who tortures Dr. Strange with

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:45.900
energy daggers.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.519
It's a haunting interaction, and you might
have guessed, it's another one that's inspired

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.040
by the comics.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:57.000
In this case, the very personal relationship
was featured in Infinity, a modern-day sequel

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:01.900
to Infinity Gauntlet that likewise served
as a major inspiration for Infinity War.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.680
Hulk in a Strange land

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:10.610
One of the movie's most striking visuals is
Hulk getting teleported to Earth to warn Dr.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:14.200
Strange and the rest of the MCU heroes that
Thanos is coming.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.761
While it's a great way to showcase Thanos'
indomitable power early in the film, it's

00:04:18.761 --> 00:04:22.610
also taken right from the original Infinity
Gauntlet comics.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:27.220
In the comics, though, it's the Silver Surfer
who gets launched into Dr. Strange's Sanctum.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.640
There's a deeper reason behind that swap of
characters than just rights issues and Hulk

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.120
being the strongest one there is.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:38.460
Hulk, Dr. Strange, and Silver Surfer are often
contemporaries in the comics as a part of

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:42.900
a super team, the Defenders, so it's not a
stretch to substitute one for the other.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:44.560
White Wolf

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:49.700
If you missed the reference at the end of
Black Panther, then Avengers: Infinity War

00:04:49.701 --> 00:04:55.470
wants to make sure that you heard right: Bucky
gets referred to as the White Wolf by T'Challa

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.780
when they're preparing to fight Thanos.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:03.010
In the comics, Hunter, the White Wolf, is
Wakanda's spymaster, as well as T'Challa's

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:04.200
caucasian adopted brother.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:09.440
With Infinity War doubling down on the reference,
it looks likely that they're establishing

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.970
that Bucky will have a place in Wakanda as
a spy following the conclusion of the battle

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.540
against Thanos... if he survives, that is.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:18.480
Ambiguous ending

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.780
Avengers: Infinity War ends with Thanos wiping
out half the universe, smiling contentedly

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.690
after accomplishing his goals.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:31.060
Interestingly enough, that ending is a direct
mirror to the original ending of Infinity

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:35.560
Gauntlet... although the comics ending comes
after Thanos' defeat at the hands of Adam

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:37.060
Warlock and most the Marvel universe.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:42.060
It's a surprisingly sympathetic ending, showing
the Mad Titan at peace with himself, having

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:47.240
had and lost ultimate power, and choosing
instead to live as a humble farmer with no

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.440
higher aspirations… for now.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:54.340
While the movie implies that the scene of
Thanos' contentment comes immediately after

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:58.120
he wipes out half of all life, that's not
necessarily true.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:03.380
Could the scene actually be a sneak-preview
of the ending of next year's Avengers movie,

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:05.880
giving fans an early look at Thanos' retirement?

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.540
Only a year to find out.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.080
Thanks for watching!

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.680
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
